  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As of 1:55 AM EDT...Current sfc analysis shows \na 1020 mb sfc high sitting centered just off the NJ coast, but \nit's close enough to have kept skies clear and winds calm for \nour region through the night so far. This has led to \ntemperatures already falling into the mid 20s for some high \nterrain areas, with valleys mainly in the 30s at this time. Some\nmid and high clouds looks to spread across northers areas \ntowards daybreak, but with several more hours of clear skies and\ncalm winds before these clouds arrive, temperatures should drop\na few to several more degrees before sunrise. Accordingly, \nfrost/freeze conditions are expected area-wide tonight, with the\npreviously issued frost advisories/freeze warnings remaining in\neffect for areas where the growing season continues. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Today warms back into the 60s under partly to \nmostly sunny skies. Southerly winds also become breezy (gusts to\naround 25 mph possible) this afternoon as the sfc high slides \noff to our east and a strengthening area of low pressure tracks \nnear James Bay. With more clouds and breezy conditions \ncontinuing tonight, lows will be warmer, mainly in the 40s, so \nno additional concerns for frost. As the cold front associated \nwith the aforementioned sfc low approaches late tonight into \ntomorrow, we could see a few light showers, especially across \nnorthern/high terrain areas. With broad upper troughing and an \nassociated cold pool aloft overhead Thursday, some additional \nshowers will be possible. We increased PoPs to at least slight \nchance for areas north of I-90 in collaboration with WFO BTV for\nThursday. Highest chances will be over the west facing slopes \nof the terrain where upslope enhancement is expected. Winds \nshift to the west Thursday behind the front, and will again be \nbreezy with some gusts to around 25 mph. \n\nThe upper trough slowly slides off to our east Friday, as sfc high \npressure builds in from the west. Increasing subsidence and low-\nlevel dry advection should result in a mainly dry day. With the sfc \nhigh overhead Friday night, effective radiational cooling could \nallow temperatures again to drop into the 30s, with some near or \nbelow freezing temps possible in the ADKs. Confidence is high that \nSaturday will remain dry with the sfc high overhead and large-\nscale subsidence. It should be a very nice fall day with highs \nwarming into the upper 60s to around 70 under an amplifying \nupper ridge. \n\nSunday into early next week...Sfc high slides off to the east \nas the remnants of what is currently Tropical Depression Nine \nin the Gulf track northeastwards towards our area. Confidence is\nlow in the exact track of this system, as well as the timing. \nConsensus is for the best chance for rain to be Sunday night \ninto Monday, but there are some ensemble members and the 00z GFS\nthat bring rain into the area during the day Sunday. At this \ntime, probability for any hydro issues looks very low with NBM \nprobs for >1\" of rain under 15% area wide and probability for \n>2\" under 5% area wide. Most of our area will be going on 1-2 \nweeks since the last appreciable rainfall by early next week, so\nany rain we receive will be much-needed.",
